From: mklab <noreply@example.invalid>
Subject: [PATCH 47/47] TODO 17.5: the build honours SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH, so the date stops being the reason

Arch-tested: x86 amd64 ppc

§17.5 recorded that rebuilding the same tree produces different bytes and that
"something dated is baked in during bootstrap", and left it there. It is
Makefile.target: it generates obj-<arch>/forth/version.fs from
`date +'%b %e %Y %H:%M'` and compiles it into the dictionary. Two builds of the
x86 tree a minute apart differ by EXACTLY TWO BYTES -- the minute digits.

THE DATE IS NOT NOISE, WHICH IS WHY IT IS NOT DELETED. smoke-openbios.sh's ppc
track proves the running firmware is OURS rather than the distro's -bios blob by
comparing exactly this banner, and boots the distro blob alongside to show the
two differ. Stamping a constant would make that comparison pass on two identical
blobs. So: reproducible ON REQUEST. SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH is the reproducible-builds
standard; unset, the behaviour is bit-for-bit what it was.

`:=`, not `=`, so the Forth and C consumers get the SAME stamp within one make
run rather than two `date` calls that can straddle a minute.

MEASURED, BOTH DIRECTIONS. With the epoch pinned to 1700000000 the x86
dictionary, openbios-builtin.elf and openbios.multiboot are byte-identical
across two builds, and the dictionary is stamped "Nov 14 2023 22:13" -- the
control proving the build honoured the variable rather than two builds merely
landing in the same minute. Without it, two builds a minute apart differ, by
those two bytes and nothing else.

AND THE CHASE FOUND A SECOND CAUSE THAT WAS NOT IN THE RECORD. With the date
pinned, the amd64 dictionary STILL differs, by ~79 bytes. The cell holding
`end-mem` reads

    build A   0000 7322 4a7d e018
    build B   0000 70d9 f729 c018

which are canonical Linux userspace addresses: a HOST POINTER is baked into the
shipped dictionary and moves with ASLR. forth/bootstrap/memory.fs's init-mem
runs on the host while the dictionary is being built and stores the host arena's
bounds; the dump keeps them. x86 does not show it -- its target cell is four
bytes and what lands there is small and stable.

That second cause is DIAGNOSED AND RECORDED, not fixed here: it is a change to
the bootstrap's memory init, which §17.5 did not ask for and which should not be
made blind at the end of a long session. tools/openbios-check-reproducible.sh
carries it as a row that MUST STILL FAIL and exits non-zero if it ever stops --
an all-PASS reproducibility check is indistinguishable from one that compares
nothing.

--- a/Makefile.target
+++ b/Makefile.target
@@ -57,16 +57,30 @@
 
 versions: $(ODIR)/target/include/openbios-version.h $(ODIR)/forth/version.fs
 
+# TODO 17.5: the build date is what makes a build non-reproducible, and it is
+# NOT noise -- smoke-openbios.sh's ppc track proves the running firmware is OURS
+# rather than the distro's -bios blob by comparing exactly this banner. Removing
+# it would delete an identity check to buy a property nothing needed.
+#
+# So: reproducible ON REQUEST. SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH is the reproducible-builds
+# standard; when it is set the stamp is derived from it, and when it is not the
+# behaviour is bit-for-bit what it always was. := not =, so both consumers below
+# get the SAME stamp within one make run rather than two `date` calls that can
+# straddle a minute.
+BUILD_DATE := $(shell if [ -n "$$SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH" ]; then \
+	LC_ALL=C TZ=UTC date -u -d "@$$SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH" +'%b %e %Y %H:%M'; \
+	else LC_ALL=C TZ=UTC date +'%b %e %Y %H:%M'; fi)
+
 $(ODIR)/forth/version.fs:
 	$(call quiet-command,true, "  GEN   $(TARGET_DIR)$@")
-	@DATE="$(shell echo `LC_ALL=C TZ=UTC date +'%b %e %Y %H:%M'`)" ;  \
+	@DATE="$(BUILD_DATE)" ;  \
 		( echo ": builddate \" $$DATE\" ; " ;     \
 		  echo ": version \" $(VERSION)\" ; " ; ) \
 		  > $(dir $@)/version.fs
 
 $(ODIR)/target/include/openbios-version.h:
 	$(call quiet-command,true, "  GEN   $(TARGET_DIR)$@")
-	@DATE="$(shell echo `LC_ALL=C TZ=UTC date +'%b %e %Y %H:%M'`)" ; \
+	@DATE="$(BUILD_DATE)" ; \
           ( echo "#define OPENBIOS_BUILD_DATE \"$$DATE\"" ; \
            echo "#define OPENBIOS_VERSION_STR \"$(VERSION)\"" ; ) \
          > $(dir $@)/openbios-version.h
